{"batchcomplete":"","query":{"normalized":[{"from":"Hand_Drill","to":"Hand Drill"},{"from":"Ice_Cream","to":"Ice Cream"},{"from":"Unceasing_Top","to":"Unceasing Top"},{"from":"Frozen_Eye","to":"Frozen Eye"},{"from":"Maw_Bank","to":"Maw Bank"},{"from":"Blood_Vial","to":"Blood Vial"},{"from":"Red_Circlet","to":"Red Circlet"},{"from":"War_Paint","to":"War Paint"},{"from":"Singing_Bowl","to":"Singing Bowl"},{"from":"Eternal_Feather","to":"Eternal Feather"},{"from":"Meal_Ticket","to":"Meal Ticket"},{"from":"Toy_Ornithopter","to":"Toy Ornithopter"},{"from":"Stone_Calendar","to":"Stone Calendar"},{"from":"Red_Skull","to":"Red Skull"},{"from":"Clockwork_Souvenir","to":"Clockwork Souvenir"},{"from":"Pure_Water","to":"Pure Water"}],"pages":{"2499":{"pageid":2499,"ns":0,"title":"Blood Vial","revisions":[{"revid":50805,"parentid":37121,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Blood Vial\n|Image       = BloodVial.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = At the start of each combat, heal 2 HP.\n|Flavor      = A vial containing the blood of a pure and elder vampire.\n}}\n{{R|Blood Vial}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|Common:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Blood Vial}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Blood Vial}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Blood Vial}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Blood Vial}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Blood Vial}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Blood Vial}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Blood Vial}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Blood Vial}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Blood Vial}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Blood Vial}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Related Events ==\n* {{R|Blood Vial}} can be offered to prevent the Max HP reduction in the {{E|Vampires}} event.\n** {{Frame|Accept}} <span class=\"positive-text\">Remove all</span> {{C|Strike (Ironclad)|Strikes}}. <span class=\"positive-text\">Receive 5</span> {{C|Bite|Bites}}. <span class=\"negative-text\">Lose (30%) Max HP.</span>\n** {{Frame|Lose Blood Vial}}* <span class=\"positive-text\"> Remove all</span> {{C|Strike (Ironclad)|Strikes}}. <span class=\"positive-text\">Receive 5</span> {{C|Bite|Bites}}. ''* (Only if {{R|Blood Vial}} has been obtained)''\n** {{Frame|Refuse}}\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Blood Vial}}'s healing occurs at the start of combat and therefore benefits from the 50% healing bonus from {{R|Magic Flower}}, healing 3 instead of the normal 2.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* As with all healing effects, {{R|Blood Vial}} restores no HP if the player possesses {{R|Mark of the Bloom}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The flavor text mentions that {{R|Blood Vial}} contains ''the blood of a pure and elder vampire''. This could be the blood of the \"Master\" that the vampires mention in the {{E|Vampires}} event.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 54: Loop'''\n** Updated relic asset for {{R|Blood Vial}}.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1928":{"pageid":1928,"ns":0,"title":"Clockwork Souvenir","revisions":[{"revid":56639,"parentid":55698,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Clockwork Souvenir\n| Image       = ClockworkSouvenir.png\n| Rarity      = Shop\n| Description = Start each combat with 1 {{BD|Artifact}}.\n| Flavor      = So many intricate gears.\n| GivesToPlayer = Artifact\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Artifact}}\n{{R|Clockwork Souvenir}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}} [[Relic]]. For more information on which effects can be prevented by {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}'s {{BD|Artifact}} (and their sources), see [[Debuffs]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be bought from the [[Merchant]]. Only 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} is available per [[Shop]], even with {{R|The Courier}}, since it cannot restock Shop Relics.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n<!-- If you decide to change this section on Mutagenic Strength in some way, please also change it in the Mutagenic Strength page for consistency! -->\n* If the player has both {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}} and {{R|Mutagenic Strength}}, the effect is dependent on the order in which the relics were obtained. The order of the effects is also somewhat counter-intuitive, and can seem \"backwards\" of their pickup order.\n** If {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}} was obtained first, {{R|Mutagenic Strength}} applies its effects before {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}. The player starts each combat with 3 {{BD|Strength Down}}, 3 {{BD|Strength}}, and 1 {{BD|Artifact}}.\n** If {{R|Mutagenic Strength}} was obtained first, the {{BD|Artifact}} from {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}} is applied first and negates the {{BD|Strength Down}} from {{R|Mutagenic Strength}}. The player starts each combat with 3 {{BD|Strength}}, which will remain for the rest of the fight.\n* {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}'s {{BD|Artifact}} will always be applied before the {{BD|Weak}} from {{R|Gremlin Visage}}, regardless of the order in which the two relics were obtained, negating it if no other [[Debuffs]] are applied in between.\n* If the player has all three of {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}, {{R|Mutagenic Strength}}, and {{R|Gremlin Visage}}, then the {{BD|Weak}} will only be prevented if {{R|Mutagenic Strength}} was acquired ''after'' {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}. It does not matter when {{R|Gremlin Visage}} was acquired relative to the other relics. Here is an explanation of the ordering and the state of the player's [[Debuffs]] depending on the relic pickup order:\n** The player will be left with 3 {{BD|Strength}} and 1 {{BD|Weak}} (no {{BD|Strength Down}}) if {{R|Mutagenic Strength}} was acquired '''before''' {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}.\n** The player will be left with 3 {{BD|Strength Down}} and 3 {{BD|Strength}} (no {{BD|Weak}}) if {{R|Mutagenic Strength}} was acquired '''after''' {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}.\n* {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}'s {{BD|Artifact}} will always be applied after the {{BD|Confused}} from {{R|Snecko Eye}}, regardless of the order in which the two relics were obtained, and can therefore never negate it.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}} can be used to prevent an important [[Debuff]] from some specific enemies, such as the negative {{BD|Dexterity}} from {{M|Lagavulin}}'s {{Int|Lagavulin|Siphon Soul}}, or the {{BD|Vulnerable}} from {{M|Gremlin Nob}}'s {{Int|Gremlin Nob|Skull Bash}} and the {{M|Corrupt Heart}}'s {{Int|Corrupt Heart|Debilitate}}.\n* {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}} can allow certain temporary [[Buffs]] to last the entire combat by preventing the [[Debuff]] that would remove the buff, such as the {{BD|Strength Down}} from {{P|Flex Potion}} and {{C|Flex}}, and the {{BD|Dexterity Down}} from {{P|Speed Potion}}.\n\n==== Positive Effects That Also Debuff the Player ====\n<!-- NOTE: If you add to (or remove from) this list, consider doing the same to Orange Pellets for consistency purposes! -->\nThe following is a list of effects which generally offer some positive benefit, but also come with some sort of [[Debuff]] that can be prevented by {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}'s {{BD|Artifact}}:\n<!-- NOTE: do not add keywords to things like \"negative Focus for Biased Cognition\" because Clockwork Souvenir will ALWAYS prevent the initial debuff (e.g. \"Bias\") before it has any opportunity to prevent the debuffs caused by that first one (e.g. the \"negative focus from Bias\"). It is impossible for * specifically* Clockwork Souvenir's Artifact to block Biased Cognition's negative Focus as it will always be consumed to block Bias first. -->\n* {{C|Flex}} ({{BD|Strength Down}})\n* {{C|Battle Trance}} ({{BD|No Draw}})\n* {{C|Berserk}} ({{BD|Vulnerable}})\n* {{C|Bullet Time}} ({{BD|No Draw}})\n* {{C|Wraith Form}} ({{BD|Wraith Form}})\n* {{C|Reprogram}} (negative {{BD|Focus}})\n* {{C|Biased Cognition}} ({{BD|Bias}})\n* {{C|Hyperbeam}} (negative {{BD|Focus}})\n* {{C|Fasting}} ({{BD|Fasting}})\n* {{C|Panic Button}} ({{BD|No Block}})\n* {{R|Duality}} ({{BD|Dexterity Down}})\n* {{R|Mutagenic Strength}} ({{BD|Strength Down}})\n* {{P|Flex Potion}} ({{BD|Strength Down}})\n* {{P|Speed Potion}} ({{BD|Dexterity Down}})\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}} does not prevent the {{BD|Blasphemer}} effect gained from playing {{C|Blasphemy}} as it is specifically implemented as a [[Buff]] to make it more difficult to avoid.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 32: Face Trader'''\n** Adding shop relic Clockwork Souvenir.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3133":{"pageid":3133,"ns":0,"title":"Enchiridion","revisions":[{"revid":55757,"parentid":51020,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Enchiridion\n|Image       = Enchiridion.png\n|Rarity      = Event\n|Source      = Cursed Tome\n|Description = At the start of each combat, add a random {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} card into your hand. It costs 0 for that turn.\n|Flavor      = The legendary journal of an ancient lich.\n|Keywords    = Hand\n}}\n{{R|Enchiridion}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\nCan only be obtained from {{E|Cursed Tome}} by choosing the {{Frame|Read}} -> {{Frame|Continue}} -> {{Frame|Continue}} -> {{Frame|Continue}} -> {{Frame|Take}} options and randomly receiving {{R|Enchiridion}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Enchiridion}} will always trigger ''before'' the player draws any cards for their opening hand. This includes the normal 5 cards drawn per turn, and card draw effects such as {{R|Ring of the Snake}} and {{R|Bag of Preparation}}.\n* {{R|Enchiridion}} will trigger with other \"before opening hand\" relics (such as {{R|Ninja Scroll}} and {{R|Toolbox}}) in pickup order (i.e. from left to right in the player's relic bar).\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Snecko Eye}}'s {{BD|Confused}} [[Debuff|debuff]] will not randomize the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power's}} {{KW|Energy}} cost and allow it to remain at 0 since the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} is directly added to the player's hand.\n* {{R|Mummified Hand}} will have a consistent and free trigger to discount another random card in the player's hand at the start of each combat.\n* {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} will have a consistent and free trigger to heal the player for 2 HP each combat.\n* {{R|Ink Bottle}} can gain a consistent and free +1 to its counter each combat. This is especially useful since the counter keeps its value between combats.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* The player may find it awkward to deal with the random {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} in the fight against {{M|Awakened One}} if {{R|Enchiridion}} gives them an undesirable {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} that they do not want to play. They must choose between dealing with continually drawing it, effectively wasting a draw, or buff the {{M|Awakened One}} due to their {{BD|Curiosity}} in order to remove the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} from their draw pile.\n\n=== Excluded Cards ===\nAs with most random card generation effects, {{R|Enchiridion}} cannot generate cards that are unobtainable from card rewards or cards that can heal the player. The following {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} will never be generated by {{R|Enchiridion}}:\n* {{C|Self Repair}} (healing)\n* {{C|Omega}} (can only be generated during combat via {{C|Alpha}} and {{C|Beta}})\n* All {{QueryLink|Cards|color:Colorless&type:Power|Colorless powers}} ({{R|Enchiridion}} will only generate {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} of the player character's color and is not affected by {{R|Prismatic Shard}})\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Enchiridion}} is a reference to [https://adventuretime.fandom.com/wiki/The_Enchiridion_(book) The Enchiridion] from the cartoon series [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adventure_Time Adventure Time].\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 52: Release Date Price Increase'''\n** Enchiridion relic now triggers earlier so its card is prioritized over some other relics.\n* '''Weekly Patch 44: Chrysalis'''\n** All sources of random temporary card generation in combat (like Dead Branch) can no longer generate cards that provide healing.\n** New tip added to indicate that healing cards cannot be generated randomly during combat.\n* '''Weekly Patch 17: Patched in the Face'''\n** Seeds are now utilized for Enchiridion and Codex.\n* '''Weekly Patch 15: Surprise Patch'''\n** Enchiridion cost should only set for one turn.\n* '''Weekly Patch 11: A Library of Features'''\n** Enchiridion now works with Snecko Eye properly.\n* '''Weekly Patch 7: Setting Up All the Things'''\n** Enchiridion relic wording improvements.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2566":{"pageid":2566,"ns":0,"title":"Eternal Feather","revisions":[{"revid":50841,"parentid":43012,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Eternal Feather\n|Image       = EternalFeather.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = For every 5 cards in your deck, heal 3 HP whenever you enter a Rest Site.\n|Flavor      = This feather appears to be completely indestructible. What bird does this possibly come from? \n|Keywords    = Deck\n}}\n{{R|Eternal Feather}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Eternal Feather}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Eternal Feather}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Eternal Feather}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Eternal Feather}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Eternal Feather}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Eternal Feather}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Eternal Feather}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Eternal Feather}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Eternal Feather}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Eternal Feather}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Eternal Feather}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n{{R|Eternal Feather}}'s healing is applied immediately upon entering a [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Site]] before any of its options are chosen.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Prayer Wheel}} can allow the player to add more cards to their deck sooner, improving the healing of {{R|Eternal Feather}}.\n* {{KW|Hoarder}} along with most [[Custom Mode]] modifiers will add more cards to your starting deck and allow you to add cards to your deck faster, making '''Eternal Feather''' heal for significantly more health.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Eternal Feather}} is likely one of {{M|Awakened One|Awakened One's}} feathers, given its blue color and HP-restoring properties (similar to {{M|Awakened One|Awakened One's}} {{BD|Regenerate}}).\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Eternal Feather relic rarity changed from Boss -> Uncommon.\n* '''Weekly Patch 53: Blizzard'''\n** Updated relic asset for Eternal Feather.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Removed extra space on flavor description for Eternal Feather.\n* '''Weekly Patch 34: Malaise'''\n** Eternal Feather relic buff: 2 -> 3 HP per 5 cards.\n* '''Weekly Patch 8: Ooh Shiny!'''\n** Wording: Consistency in description for Ancient Tea Set and Eternal Feather.\n* '''Weekly Patch 4: Full speed ahead!'''\n** Eternal Feather grammatical fix.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3146":{"pageid":3146,"ns":0,"title":"Frozen Eye","revisions":[{"revid":50958,"parentid":37166,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Frozen Eye\n|Image       = FrozenEye.png\n|Rarity      = Shop\n|Description = When viewing your Draw Pile, the cards are now shown in order.\n|Flavor      = Staring into the eye, you see a glimpse of your future.\n}}\n{{R|Frozen Eye}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be bought from the [[Merchant]]. Only 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} is available per [[Shop]], even with {{R|The Courier}}, since it cannot restock Shop Relics.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Any effect that draws cards will typically know which cards would be added to the player's hand. This can be used to decide whether any {{KW|Energy}} spent on card draw effects (such as {{C|Pommel Strike}}, {{C|Acrobatics}}, {{C|Coolheaded}}, and {{C|Wheel Kick}}) or using {{P|Swift Potion}} would be worth the cost or not.\n** This also means the player is usually able to know their hand for the next turn.\n* {{R|Frozen Eye}} allows the player to see if {{C|Escape Plan}} will grant {{KW|Block}} by drawing a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skill}} or not.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* Since only the draw pile is viewable in order, {{R|Frozen Eye}} is of no help if the draw pile is empty or does not contain enough cards to fully satisfy an instance of card draw because the player's discard pile will shuffle and become their new draw pile. There is no way for the player to know what extra cards will be drawn in this scenario.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 45: Impatience'''\n** Draw Pile no longer indicates that it is a shuffled view order when viewing it with Frozen Eye.\n* '''Weekly Patch 44: Chrysalis'''\n** New Daily Mod - Controlled Chaos. Start with Frozen Eye. At the start of your turn, add 10 random cards to the bottom of your draw pile.\n* '''Weekly Patch 17: Patched in the Face'''\n** Frozen Eye is now a shop relic. (was rare)\n* '''Weekly Patch 4: Full speed ahead!'''\n** Adding new relic Frozen Eye.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3147":{"pageid":3147,"ns":0,"title":"Hand Drill","revisions":[{"revid":55812,"parentid":50961,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Hand Drill\n|Image       = HandDrill.png\n|Rarity      = Shop\n|Description = Whenever you break an enemy's {{KW|Block}}, apply 2 {{BD|Vulnerable}}.\n|Flavor      = Spirals are dangerous.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Block}} {{Power Infobox|Vulnerable}}\n{{R|Hand Drill}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be bought from the [[Merchant]]. Only 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} is available per [[Shop]], even with {{R|The Courier}}, since it cannot restock Shop Relics.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* When calculating damage dealt, all the additive bonuses are added first, and then multiplicative bonuses are multiplied. The final result is rounded down. {{R|Hand Drill|Hand Drill's}} {{BD|Vulnerable}} is a multiplicative bonus.\n* If an enemy's {{KW|Block}} is broken from a multi-hit {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}} (such as {{C|Pummel}} or {{C|Riddle with Holes}}), {{R|Hand Drill}} will trigger adding {{BD|Vulnerable}} ''after'' the attack has completely finished all of its hits.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Paper Phrog}} will increase the damage bonus of {{BD|Vulnerable}} from 50% to 75%.\n* {{R|Champion Belt}} will apply 1 {{BD|Weak}} to the enemy whenever {{R|Hand Drill}} triggers and adds {{BD|Vulnerable}}.\n\n=== Anti Synergies ===\n* {{C|Melter}} will not trigger {{R|Hand Drill}} as it does not ''break'' the enemy's {{KW|Block}} but instead ''removes it'' entirely.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Hand Drill}} is a reference to the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mecha_anime_and_manga mecha anime] [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gurren_Lagann Gurren Lagann] where the main character is seen using a small hand-drill to expand his subterranean village.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 33: Terror'''\n** Adding relic Hand Drill.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1955":{"pageid":1955,"ns":0,"title":"Ice Cream","revisions":[{"revid":56779,"parentid":50883,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Ice Cream\n|Image       = IceCream.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = {{KW|Energy}} is now conserved between turns.\n|Flavor      = Delicious!\n|InteractsWith = Energy\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}\n{{R|Ice Cream}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]]. It prevents the player's {{KW|Energy}} from being reset at the start of each turn, and instead adds the maximum energy limit (typically 3, but can increase from certain relics) to the player's current energy at the start of each turn.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Ice Cream}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Ice Cream}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Ice Cream}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Ice Cream}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Ice Cream}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Ice Cream}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Ice Cream}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Ice Cream}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Ice Cream}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Ice Cream}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Ice Cream}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Ice Cream}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* One-turn {{KW|Energy}}-generating effects (such as {{R|Ancient Tea Set}}, {{R|Happy Flower}}, and {{R|Lantern}}) can have their extra {{KW|Energy}} saved for later turns.\n* {{KW|Energy}}-generating cards and relics (such as {{C|Double Energy}}, {{R|Sundial}}, {{C|Bloodletting}}, and {{C|Adrenaline}}) can have their extra {{KW|Energy}} saved for later turns.\n* X-cost cards (such as {{C|Whirlwind}}, {{C|Skewer}}, {{C|Multi-Cast}}, and {{C|Conjure Blade}}) scale massively with stored {{KW|Energy}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* Provides no benefit in fights where {{KW|Energy}} is consistently used every turn.\n* Less impactful in short combats where turns rarely extend long enough to stockpile or use excess {{KW|Energy}}.\n* Many {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss relics}} provide 1 additional {{KW|Energy}} at the start of each turn. Depending on the player's deck and overall run state, this extra {{KW|Energy}} can either be very useful to preserve more of it for later turns, or the player will have already mostly solved their {{KW|Energy}} needs without it. The player must carefully consider whether the drawbacks of these relics is worth the {{KW|Energy}} gain compared to other solutions and relying on {{R|Ice Cream}}'s ability to preserve {{KW|Energy}} for important turns.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* Effects that limit cards played (such as {{R|Velvet Choker}} or {{C|Normality}}) may prevent stored {{KW|Energy}} from being used effectively.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Ice Cream}} depicts a 3-scoop cone of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neapolitan_ice_cream Neapolitan ice cream]; a type of ice cream separated into 3 distinct sections of vanilla, chocolate, and strawberry ice cream flavors.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2536":{"pageid":2536,"ns":0,"title":"Mango","revisions":[{"revid":50886,"parentid":37193,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Mango\n|Image       = Mango.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = Upon pickup, raise your Max HP by 14.\n|Flavor      = The most coveted forgotten fruit. Impeccably preserved with no signs of Spireblight.\n}}\n{{R|Mango}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Mango}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Mango}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Mango}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Mango}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Mango}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Mango}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Mango}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Mango}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Mango}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Mango}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Mango}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Mango}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* If {{R|Mango}} is removed by {{E|N'loth}}, the player's Max HP will not decrease.\n\n=== Anti-Synergy ===\n* Gaining more Max HP is mostly worthless if the player has {{R|Mark of the Bloom}} which prevents all healing. It also prevents the behavior where gaining Max HP will restore HP equal to the amount of Max HP gained.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Mango}} is the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} rarity relic in the \"fruit relic set\" which all increase Max HP on pickup; the others being the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} {{R|Strawberry}} and the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} {{R|Pear}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 25: Building Blocks'''\n** HP-gain relics (Fruit & Waffle) now have consistent pickup wording.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1798":{"pageid":1798,"ns":0,"title":"Maw Bank","revisions":[{"revid":55459,"parentid":50813,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Maw Bank\n|Image       = MawBank.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Whenever you climb a floor, gain 12 [[Gold]]. No longer works when you spend any [[Gold]] at a shop.\n|Flavor      = Surprisingly popular, despite maw attacks being a regular occurrence.\n}}\n{{R|Maw Bank}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Maw Bank}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Maw Bank}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Maw Bank}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Maw Bank}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Maw Bank}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Maw Bank}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Maw Bank}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Maw Bank}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Maw Bank}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Maw Bank}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n* Cannot be bought from the [[Merchant]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n* {{R|Maw Bank}} will not break when spending [[Gold]] at an [[Event]].\n* {{R|Maw Bank}} will trigger its [[Gold]] gain on floors containing a [[Mechanics#Chests|Boss Chest]].\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Maw Bank}} will trigger as soon as a map node is selected.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Combined with the {{R|Bloody Idol}}, the player will restore 5 HP at the start of each floor.\n* The purchasing power (or \"effective gold\") at [[Merchant|Shops]] granted by {{R|Maw Bank}} increases if the player has {{R|The Courier}} and/or {{R|Membership Card}}.\n<!--\nBe very careful with the math if you decide rewrite this section.\nThe correct formula for purchasing power (or \"effective gold\") is [  12 / (1 - discount)  ] and NOT [  12 * (1 + discount)  ].\nA 50% discount should DOUBLE the purchasing power (+100%)! It's not just a 50% increase.\n-->\n** {{R|The Courier|The Courier's}} 20% discount makes the 12 [[Gold]] from {{R|Maw Bank}} have the purchasing power of 15 [[Gold]].\n** {{R|Membership Card|Membership Card's}} 50% discount makes the 12 [[Gold]] from {{R|Maw Bank}} have the purchasing power of 24 [[Gold]], but as a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} it must be bought from the [[Merchant|Shop]] prior to acquiring {{R|Maw Bank}}.\n** Having both {{R|The Courier}} and {{R|Membership Card}} is a total 60% discount. This makes the 12 [[Gold]] from {{R|Maw Bank}} have the purchasing power of 30 [[Gold]].\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{R|Wing Boots}} can allow the player to avoid the [[Merchant]], potentially saving more [[Gold]] in the {{R|Maw Bank}}.\n* {{R|Juzu Bracelet}} increases the likelihood of encountering the [[Merchant]], which may break the {{R|Maw Bank}}.\n\n=== Anti Synergies ===\n* {{R|Ectoplasm}} will negate any [[Gold]] gained, making an active {{R|Maw Bank}} effectively stop functioning.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* This relic is based off of a piggy bank, but uses {{M|The Maw}} instead.\n* {{R|Maw Bank}} will turn grey after [[Gold]] is spent at a [[Merchant]].\n* The average cost to purchase a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|common}} relic is 150 ({{Asc|16|165}}) [[Gold]]. This means that roughly 13 ({{Asc|16|14}}) floors must be ascended without spending [[Gold]] at a [[Merchant]] in order to afford another common relic using only the funds gained from {{R|Maw Bank}}.\n** With {{R|The Courier}}, this changes to roughly 10 ({{Asc|16|11}}) floors.\n** With {{R|Membership Card}}, this changes to roughly 7 ({{Asc|16|still 7}}) floors.\n** With both {{R|The Courier}} and {{R|Membership Card}}, this changes to roughly 5 ({{Asc|16|6}}) floors.\n* Developer Casey Yano has stated he enjoys the relic art of {{R|Maw Bank}} for its storytelling and mood setting. ''(citation needed)''\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Relics which gray out are now grayed out using a shader, rather than a grayscale image.\n* '''Weekly Patch 52: Release Date Price Increase'''\n** Maw Bank relic will no longer spawn at Shops.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Maw Bank relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 34: Malaise'''\n** Adding common relic Maw Bank\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2522":{"pageid":2522,"ns":0,"title":"Meal Ticket","revisions":[{"revid":55460,"parentid":50814,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Meal Ticket\n|Image       = MealTicket.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Whenever you enter a shop, heal 15 HP.\n|Flavor      = Complimentary meatballs with every visit!\n}}\n{{R|Meal Ticket}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|Common:Common|Common}} [[Relic]]. The player does not need to open the shop's inventory to trigger the healing effect since it happens automatically upon entering the [[Merchant|Shop]] floor. The effect triggers regardless of whether the floor was explicitly marked as a shop or occurs randomly from an [[Map Locations#Unknown|Unknown]] floor.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Meal Ticket}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Meal Ticket}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Meal Ticket}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Meal Ticket}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Meal Ticket}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Meal Ticket}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Meal Ticket}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Meal Ticket}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Meal Ticket}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Meal Ticket}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Wing Boots}} can help path into [[Merchant|Shops]] and trigger the healing effect if no [[Map Locations#Rest Site|Rest Sites]] are available and healing is needed.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{R|Meal Ticket}} can show up at a [[Merchant|Shop]], but will not trigger its effect on purchase as it only triggers immediately upon ''entering'' the floor with a '''Shop''' on it.\n* {{R|Meal Ticket}} can never have its healing improved by {{R|Magic Flower}} since a [[Merchant|Shop]] floor can never be \"during combat\".\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* As with all healing effects, {{R|Meal Ticket}} restores no HP if the player possesses {{R|Mark of the Bloom}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The flavor text ''Complimentary meatballs with every visit!'' could be a reference to the Swedish furniture store chain [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IKEA IKEA] which is known for the highly popular \"Swedish Meatballs\" served in the cafeteria of their many locations worldwide.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Meal Ticket relic rarity changed from Shop -> Common.\n* '''Patch V1.1: The Dealer'''\n** Fixed Meal Ticket not working on Shops from ? rooms.\n* '''Weekly Patch 55: Blur'''\n** Meal Ticket relic buffed. Heals 10 -> 15.\n** Meal Ticket relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (unless Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 44: Chrysalis'''\n** Meal Ticket relic buff: Heals 7 HP -> 10 HP.\n** Localization: Mutagenic Strength, Meal Ticket, and Abacus now support more flexible descriptions.\n* '''Weekly Patch 32: Face Trader'''\n** Adding shop relic Meal Ticket.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1525":{"pageid":1525,"ns":0,"title":"Pure Water","revisions":[{"revid":56355,"parentid":50799,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Pure Water\n| Image       = PureWater.png\n| Rarity      = Starter\n| Character   = Watcher\n| Description = At the start of each combat, add 1 {{C|Miracle}} into your hand.\n| Flavor      = Filtered through fine sand and free of impurities.\n}}\n{{Small Card Infobox|Miracle}}\n{{R|Pure Water}} is the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Starter|Starter}} [[Relic]] of the [[Watcher]]; they will always start every new run with it in their possession and it can't be obtained by any other character.\n\nIt can be upgraded into the {{R|Holy Water}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relic}} if it's found in a Boss Chest.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{C|Miracle}} is a convenient trigger for {{C|Crush Joints}} and {{C|Sanctity}}.\n* {{C|Miracle}} can be used on-demand to increase the effectiveness of X-cost cards (such as {{C|Collect}} and {{C|Conjure Blade}}).\n* {{C|Miracle}} is a fully reliable +1 to the counter for {{R|Ink Bottle}} every combat.\n* Increases {{KW|Block}} gained by {{R|Cloak Clasp}} and {{C|Spirit Shield}} while the {{C|Miracle}} is {{KW|Retain|retained}}.\n* Generates a card from {{R|Dead Branch}} when the {{C|Miracle}} is played, potentially being able to use the extra {{Icon|PE}}[[Keywords#Energy|energy]] to play the card that generated.\n* {{R|Ice Cream}} can preserve the {{Icon|PE}}[[Keywords#Energy|energy]] from the {{C|Miracle}} between turns without clogging the hand from too many {{KW|Retain}} cards.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* Most forms of creating {{C|Miracle}}s happen at the start of combat or enter the hand already {{KW|Upgrade|upgraded}} (in the case of {{C|Collect}}), making {{C|Master Reality}} not as useful for '''Miracles''' as it may seem at first.\n\n=== Anti-Synergy ===\n* It can be awkward to play the {{C|Miracle}} for {{Icon|PE}}[[Keywords#Energy|energy]] while {{R|Velvet Choker}} limits the number of cards that can be played in a turn.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The flavor text of {{R|Pure Water}} references [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sand_filter sand filtration]; the process of removing impurities from water by passing it through layers of fine sand granules.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** CONTENT: Watcher only, Starter Relic: Pure Water.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1723":{"pageid":1723,"ns":0,"title":"Red Circlet","revisions":[{"revid":51003,"parentid":37238,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Red Circlet\n|Image       = RedCirclet.png\n|Rarity      = Special\n|Description = You ran out of relics. Impressive!\n|Flavor      = Looks very pretty.\n}}\n{{R|Red Circlet}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Special|Special}} [[Relic]] that is supposed to be dropped when the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relic}} pool is exhausted but is unobtainable in the game due to a logic bug in the code. The only way to obtain this is through modding.\n\n{{R|Circlet}} is the obtainable variant of this Relic.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1440":{"pageid":1440,"ns":0,"title":"Red Skull","revisions":[{"revid":56326,"parentid":56041,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Red Skull\n| Image       = RedSkull.png\n| Rarity      = Common\n| Character   = Ironclad\n| Description = While your HP is at or below 50%, you have 3 additional {{BD|Strength}}.\n| Flavor      = A small skull covered in ornamental paint.\n| GivesToPlayer = Strength\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Strength}}\n{{R|Red Skull}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Ironclad|Ironclad-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]]. As with most rounding operations, the 50% is rounded down to the nearest whole number to determine when the player gains {{BD|Strength}} during combat. For example, if the player has 75 Max HP, {{R|Red Skull}} will trigger when the player has 37 current HP.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Red Skull}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Red Skull}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Red Skull}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Red Skull}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Red Skull}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Red Skull}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Red Skull}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Red Skull}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Red Skull}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Red Skull}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Ironclad]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* When calculating damage dealt, all the additive bonuses are added first, and then multiplicative bonuses are multiplied. The final result is rounded down. {{R|Red Skull}}'s {{BD|Strength}} is an additive bonus.\n* The {{BD|Strength}} is gained immediately after the player's current HP changes, even when it's not their turn.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n{{BD|Strength}} is a widely applicable synergy that increases the damage of all {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}}. Only the most notable examples are shown here.\n\n* {{BD|Strength}} counts towards additional HP restored by {{C|Reaper}}.\n* {{BD|Strength}} counts towards additional {{KW|Block}} given by {{C|Wallop}}.\n* {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} that deal damage to all enemies (such as {{C|Cleave}} and {{C|Conclude}}) will apply {{BD|Strength|Strengths}} damage bonus to all affected enemies.\n* Multi-hit attacks benefit from {{BD|Strength}} for each hit.\n** {{C|Pummel}} deals a total of 20 (25) damage.\n** {{C|Sword Boomerang}} deals a total of 18 (24) damage.\n** {{C|Twin Strike}} deals a total of 16 (20) damage.\n** {{C|Whirlwind}} deals a total of 8X (11X) damage to all enemies.\n** {{C|Dagger Spray}} deals a total of 14 (18) damage to all enemies.\n** {{C|Eviscerate}} deals a total of 30 (36) damage.\n** {{C|Riddle with Holes}} deals a total of 30 (35) damage.\n** {{C|Skewer}} deals a total of 10X (13X) damage.\n** {{C|Barrage}} deals a total of 7N (9N) damage. (N = number of channelled orbs)\n** {{C|Rip and Tear}} deals a total of 20 (24) damage.\n** {{C|Thunder Strike}} deals a total of 10N (12N) damage. (N = number of {{KW|Lightning}} Orbs channelled)\n** {{C|Flying Sleeves}} deals a total of 14 (18) damage.\n** {{C|Tantrum}} deals a total of 18 (24) damage.\n** {{C|Ragnarok}} deals a total of 40 (54) damage.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* When paired with {{R|Meat on the Bone}} or {{R|Black Blood}}, the player must maintain a much lower HP total during a fight if they wish to trigger {{R|Red Skull}} at the start their next combat.\n\n== Bugs ==\n* The {{BD|Strength}} is removed if the player heals above 50% during combat. However, this is implemented as an \"invisible [[Debuff]]\" and can be blocked by {{BD|Artifact}}. {{R|Red Skull}} can then trigger again normally if the player falls back down to 50% HP in the same combat, leaving them with 6 {{BD|Strength}}. {{R|Orange Pellets}} does not prevent or undo this removal in the same way as the {{BD|Artifact}}, however.<ref>This interaction was most recently highlighted [https://www.reddit.com/r/slaythespire/comments/1lahgkt/til_during_combat_when_you_have_red_skull/ in a post by u/Adrianpih on Reddit], but has been posted several other times such as [https://www.reddit.com/r/slaythespire/comments/17m3ijc/comment/k7i6zlc/ in the Daily Discussion (488/696) for Red Skull]. For the sake of this wiki page, it was [https://imgur.com/a/red-skull-artifact-console-debugging-FT8bAy3# tested again in BaseMod using the console debugger] and confirmed to be still present in the most recent patch of the game; <code>[V2.3.4](12-18-2022)</code>.</ref>\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Red Skull}} is a direct reference to the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marvel_Comics Marvel] super-villain of the same name from the ''Captain America'' comics series.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Skull</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 52: Release Date Price Increase'''\n** Updated relic asset for Red Skull.\n* '''Weekly Patch 50: The Final Act'''\n** Red Skull relic's Strength gain is now correctly applied to the cards in your hand at start of combats.\n* '''Weekly Patch 44: Chrysalis'''\n** BUG FIX: Red Skull relic now correctly loses bonus strength when healing above 50% HP.\n* '''Weekly Patch 41: Chill'''\n** Red Skull relic should now correctly deactivate when healing in all scenarios.\n* '''Weekly Patch 37: Darkness'''\n** Red Skull relic no longer has odd behavior if you heal at the start of combat (Pantograph, etc).\n* '''Weekly Patch 33: Terror'''\n** Red Skull relic is now Common.\n* '''Weekly Patch 5: Happy Holidays!'''\n** <50% hp logic becomes a bit more consistent, potential fix for Red Skull not triggering.\n** Meat on the Bone, Red Skull, Slime's Split, and Berserk all trigger on less than or equal to 50% HP and desc are updated to reflect this\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2401":{"pageid":2401,"ns":0,"title":"Singing Bowl","revisions":[{"revid":55499,"parentid":50858,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Singing Bowl\n|Image       = SingingBowl.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = When adding cards to your deck, you may raise your Max HP by 2 instead.\n|Flavor      = This well-used artifact rings out with a beautiful melody when struck.\n|Keywords    = Deck\n}}\n{{R|Singing Bowl}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]]. In addition to the usual ''Skip'' button when presented with a card reward, a second button ''+2 Max HP'' is presented. The player is technically able to Skip the card reward normally without triggering {{R|Singing Bowl}}'s Max HP bonus, although the reasons for doing so are niche.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Singing Bowl}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Singing Bowl}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Singing Bowl}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Singing Bowl}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Singing Bowl}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Singing Bowl}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Singing Bowl}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Singing Bowl}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Singing Bowl}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Singing Bowl}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Singing Bowl}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n\nOnly card rewards that would permanently add a card to the player's deck are affected by {{R|Singing Bowl}}. Effects will generally mention ''\"obtain a card\"'' or specifically adding a card ''\"to your deck\"'' when they acts as card rewards, while effects that '''don't''' act as card rewards will always be phrased like ''\"choose 1 of 3 cards\"'' while in combat.\n\nFor completeness, all scenarios that seem like they could be considered a card reward during normal gameplay are listed in the sections below depending on whether they can be skipped to gain Max HP from {{R|Singing Bowl}} or not.\n\n=== Affected Card Rewards ===\n\nThe following card reward screens are affected by {{R|Singing Bowl}} and will present the ''+2 Max HP'' option to the player:\n\n* End-of-combat card rewards from defeating [[Monsters|normal enemies]].\n* End-of-combat card rewards from defeating [[Elites|elites]].\n** Unlike {{R|Question Card}}, the card reward from defeating an [[Elite]] is affected by {{R|Singing Bowl}} and can be skipped for +2 Max HP after the player picks up the relic.\n* The guaranteed {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare}} card reward from defeating a [[Map Locations#Boss|Boss]] at the end of [[Acts|Act]] 1 and 2.\n* {{R|Dream Catcher}}'s card reward after resting at a [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Site]].\n* {{R|Tiny House}}'s card reward after acquiring it.\n* {{R|Prayer Wheel}}'s extra card reward from defeating [[Monsters|normal enemies]]. Up to 4 Max HP per normal enemy encounter defeated if both card rewards are skipped.\n* {{R|Orrery}}'s card rewards after acquiring it. Up to 10 Max HP if all card rewards are skipped.\n* {{E|Sensory Stone}}'s 1-3 card rewards. Because gaining Max HP also restores HP equal to the amount of Max HP gained, the Max HP can somewhat counteract the damage the player must take in order to see the extra card rewards. 5 damage can become net 1 damage if both card rewards are skipped, and 10 damage can become net 4 damage if all three card rewards are skipped.\n\n=== Unaffected Card Rewards ===\n\nThe following card reward screens are '''not''' affected by {{R|Singing Bowl}} and cannot be skipped for +2 Max HP:\n\n* {{C|Foreign Influence}}'s card reward when played.\n* {{C|Discovery}}'s card reward when played.\n* {{R|Toolbox}}'s card reward at the start of combat.\n* {{R|Nilry's Codex}}'s card reward at the end of each turn in combat.\n* {{P|Attack Potion}}, {{P|Skill Potion}}, and {{P|Power Potion}}'s card rewards when consumed.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Dream Catcher}}, {{R|Prayer Wheel}}, {{R|Orrery}}, {{R|Tiny House}}, and {{E|Sensory Stone}}'s card rewards can all be skipped for Max HP.\n* {{R|Busted Crown}} can make card rewards less appealing, in turn making the Max HP gain more desirable.\n\n=== Anti-synergies ===\n* Gaining more Max HP is mostly worthless if the player has {{R|Mark of the Bloom}} which prevents all healing. It also prevents the behavior where gaining Max HP will restore HP equal to the amount of Max HP gained.\n* {{R|Question Card}} makes card rewards potentially offer better cards, which can make the Max HP gain less desirable.\n* {{R|Molten Egg}}, {{R|Toxic Egg}}, and {{R|Frozen Egg}} showing the player more {{KW|Upgrade|upgraded}} cards, which can make the Max HP gain less desirable.\n* {{KW|Vintage}} drastically reduces the amount of card rewards the player will see, which provides less opportunities for {{R|Singing Bowl}} to trigger.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* A singing bowl is a metallic bowl-shaped bell instrument used in some Buddhist religious practices to accompany meditation or chanting.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standing_bell</ref>\n** The generic term of this instrument is a \"standing bell\". All standing bells can be played by striking them with a mallet to produce a musical note. The \"singing bowl\" form of standing bells are somewhat unique in that they can also be played by dragging a leather-bound mallet head around its rim to produce a continuous musical note.\n** The sound effect that plays when {{R|Singing Bowl}} is triggered is the \"striking\" form of the sound that all standing bells can produce, and not the continuous musical note unique to singing bowls.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 56: The Collector'''\n** (Controllers) Button prompt for Singing Bowl's +2 Max HP button now dynamically placed so it doesn't overlap text.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Singing Bowl relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 46: Adrenaline'''\n** Hitting ESC with Singing Bowl no longer automatically chooses the Singing Bowl option on card select screens.\n** Hitting ESC with Singing Bowl when viewing an upgraded card in a reward screen no longer causes softlock.\n* '''Weekly Patch 33: Terror'''\n** Singing Bowl relic is now Uncommon. (was common)\n* '''Weekly Patch 12: Lunar New Year'''\n** Add Twitch streaming support for Singing Bowl.\n** Fixed issue where Max HP +2 button may appear if you had Singing Bowl a previous run.\n* '''Weekly Patch 11: A Library of Features'''\n** Fixed bug where Singing Bowl's +2 HP option appeared for Codex if you opened the map while viewing the cards.\n* Weekly Patch 5: Happy Holidays!\n** Singing Bowl flavor text typo fix.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3123":{"pageid":3123,"ns":0,"title":"Stone Calendar","revisions":[{"revid":55958,"parentid":50892,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Stone Calendar\n|Image       = StoneCalendar.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = At the end of turn 7, deal 52 damage to ALL enemies.\n|Flavor      = The passage of time is imperceptible in the Spire.\n}}\n{{R|Stone Calendar}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]]. At the end of turn 7, it deals 52 damage to all enemies. The damage is not repeated for turns that are multiples of 7, it is only at the end of specifically turn 7. This damage is not treated as {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} damage, and therefore does not benefit from effects like {{BD|Strength}} or {{BD|Vulnerable}}.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Stone Calendar}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Stone Calendar}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Stone Calendar}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Stone Calendar}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Stone Calendar}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Stone Calendar}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Stone Calendar}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Stone Calendar}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Stone Calendar}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Stone Calendar}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Stone Calendar}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Stone Calendar}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Stone Calendar}} triggers before {{C|Combust}}, so no HP will be lost if the relic's damage ends the fight.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The numbers in {{R|Stone Calendar}}'s effect are references to the real-world calendar; 7 turns represents the 7 days in a week, and 52 damage represents the 52 weeks in a year.\n* The carvings in {{R|Stone Calendar}} are the same runes that can be seen on {{R|Runic Cube}}, {{R|Runic Dome}}, and {{R|Runic Pyramid}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 55: Blur'''\n** Stone Calendar relic buffed. Damage 50 -> 52 for flavor.\n** Improvements to counter on Stone Calendar to make it more obvious when it will trigger.\n* '''Weekly Patch 54: Loop'''\n** Stone Calendar relic damage buffed 40 -> 50.\n* '''Weekly Patch 35: Aggregate'''\n** Adding rare relic Stone Calendar.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3130":{"pageid":3130,"ns":0,"title":"Torii","revisions":[{"revid":55982,"parentid":50895,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Torii\n|Image       = Torii.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = Whenever you would receive 5 or less unblocked {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} damage, reduce it to 1.\n|Flavor      = Holding the small Torii, you feel a sense of calm and safety drift through your mind.\n}}\n{{R|Torii}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Torii}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Torii}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Torii}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Torii}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Torii}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Torii}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Torii}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Torii}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Torii}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Torii}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Torii}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Torii}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Torii}} triggers before {{R|Tungsten Rod}}.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Torii}} and {{R|Tungsten Rod}} work together, such that any unblocked attack damage less than or equal to 5 will become 0. When 6 unblocked attack damage is taken, {{R|Torii}} does '''not''' trigger and then {{R|Tungsten Rod}} reduces the damage from 6 to 5.\n* {{R|Torii}} works well on multi-hit attacks as each instance of unblocked attack damage can have its damage reduced by the relic's effect.\n** For example, against a {{M|Byrd}}'s buffed {{Int|Byrd|Peck}} attack dealing 3x5 for 15 damage ({{Asc|2|3x6 for 18 damage}}), {{R|Torii}} will effectively reduce the attack to 1x5 for 5 damage ({{Asc|2|1x6 for 6 damage}}).\n** {{R|Torii}} works particularly well against the {{M|Corrupt Heart}}'s {{Int|Corrupt Heart|Blood Shots}} multi-attack as its damage will stay at 5 or below for the first couple times this attack is used. When combined with {{R|Tungsten Rod}}, these two relics can end up reducing a devastating 75 damage to 0 damage without needing to play any defensive cards.\n* Effects that apply {{BD|Weak}} (such as {{C|Shockwave}}, {{C|Crippling Cloud}}, and {{R|Red Mask}}) or reduce enemy {{BD|Strength}} (such as {{C|Disarm}}, {{C|Piercing Wail}}, and {{C|Dark Shackles}}) can reduce enemy damage to be within {{R|Torii}}'s effect range. This can be a very effective way to reduce massive incoming damage to only a few small points of chip damage instead.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* A torii is a traditional Japanese gate typically found at the entrances of Shinto shrines. These gates symbolically mark the transition from the mundane world into sacred land.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torii</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 2: Slimes!'''\n** FlameBarrier, Flight, Malleable, and Torii now have more checks to ensure that their effects trigger correctly.\n* '''Weekly Patch 1: The First of Many!'''\n** Torii gets a description that reflects what it does.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2539":{"pageid":2539,"ns":0,"title":"Toy Ornithopter","revisions":[{"revid":50828,"parentid":37282,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Toy Ornithopter\n|Image       = ToyOrnithopter.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Whenever you use a [[Potions|potion]], heal 5 HP.\n|Flavor      = \"This little toy is the perfect companion for the lone adventurer!\"\n}}\n{{R|Toy Ornithopter}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|Common:Common|Common}} [[Relic]]. It heals 5 HP whenever a [[Potions|potion]] is used. Discarding a potion will not grant the heal.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Toy Ornithopter}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Toy Ornithopter}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Toy Ornithopter}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Toy Ornithopter}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Toy Ornithopter}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Toy Ornithopter}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Toy Ornithopter}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Toy Ornithopter}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Toy Ornithopter}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Toy Ornithopter}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Magic Flower}} will increase the healing to 8 HP whenever a potion is used (as long as the potion is used in combat before the reward screen appears).\n* Potion-generating effects, such as {{R|White Beast Statue}} and {{C|Alchemize}}, can greatly increase the amount of HP restored by using more potions.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* As with all healing effects, {{R|Toy Ornithopter}} restores no HP if the player possesses {{R|Mark of the Bloom}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* An ornithopter is a type of aircraft that flies by flapping its wings.\n* The name ornithopter comes from the Ancient Greek words \"\u00f3rnis\" (meaning \"bird\") and \"pter\u00f3n\" (meaning \"wing\").<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ornithopter</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Toy Ornithopter relic Common changed from Shop -> Common.\n* '''Weekly Patch 54: Loop'''\n** Fixed issue where Toy Ornithopter relic wouldn't trigger outside of combat.\n* '''Weekly Patch 39: We Meet Again!'''\n** Toy Ornithopter relic buffed: Healing amount 3 -> 5.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1835":{"pageid":1835,"ns":0,"title":"Turnip","revisions":[{"revid":56387,"parentid":55987,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Turnip\n| Image       = Turnip.png\n| Rarity      = Rare\n| Description = You can no longer become {{BD|Frail}}.\n| Flavor      = Best with Ginger.\n| InteractsWith = Frail\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Frail}}\n{{R|Turnip}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Turnip}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Turnip}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Turnip}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Turnip}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Turnip}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Turnip}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Turnip}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Turnip}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Turnip}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Turnip}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Turnip}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Turnip}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Turnip}} will trigger before {{BD|Artifact}} whenever {{BD|Frail}} would be applied. Or in other words, no {{BD|Artifact}} will be consumed if the player would receive {{BD|Frail}} while possessing {{R|Turnip}}.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Turnip}} can help decks that rely on mitigating damage using {{KW|Block}} cards (such as {{C|Impervious}}, {{C|Leg Sweep}}, {{C|Equilibrium}}, and {{C|Spirit Shield}}) against enemies that apply {{BD|Frail}} (such as {{M|Shelled Parasite}} and {{M|The Collector}}).\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{BD|Frail}} only reduces {{KW|Block}} gained from cards that grant {{KW|Block}} when played. As such, {{R|Turnip}} is not needed for many other {{KW|Block}} gain effects, such as {{C|Feel No Pain}}, {{C|After Image}}, {{KW|Frost|Frost Orbs}}, {{C|Talk to the Hand}}, and {{P|Block Potion}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Turnips are root vegetables known for their white fleshy taproot, nutrient-rich greens, and slight purple coloration near the stem.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turnip</ref>\n** Turnip greens are a very rich source of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitamin_K vitamin K], which is used by the body to aid in blood coagulation and bone health. It's possible that this is the inspiration for {{R|Turnip}}'s effect.\n* {{R|Turnip}} is one of the relics in the \"edible relic set\" that all reduce [[Debuffs|debuffs]]; the others being {{R|Ginger}} for blocking {{BD|Weak}} and {{R|Odd Mushroom}} for lessening the effectiveness of {{BD|Vulnerable}}.\n* {{R|Turnip}} is in the fourth set of unlocks for the [[Defect]].\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 27'''\n** Content: 9 Relics. (including Turnip)\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1831":{"pageid":1831,"ns":0,"title":"Unceasing Top","revisions":[{"revid":56384,"parentid":50898,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Unceasing Top\n| Image       = UnceasingTop.png\n| Rarity      = Rare\n| Description = Whenever you have no cards in hand during your turn, draw a card.\n| Flavor      = The top continues to spin effortlessly as if you were in a dream.\n| GivesToPlayer = Draw\n}}\n{{R|Unceasing Top}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Unceasing Top}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Unceasing Top}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Unceasing Top}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Unceasing Top}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Unceasing Top}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Unceasing Top}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Unceasing Top}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Unceasing Top}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Unceasing Top}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Unceasing Top}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Unceasing Top}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Unceasing Top}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n{{R|Unceasing Top}} will trigger whenever the player's hand has no cards in it '''''after''''' any ongoing effects have resolved. In other words, {{R|Unceasing Top}} will check if it should trigger ''right before'' control is handed back to the player to play their next card. This can be unintuitive at first, but some sense can be made with a few examples:\n\n==== Scenarios That Trigger {{R|Unceasing Top}} ====\n* When the player's hand is empty after playing a \"normal\" card with no effects that would immediately change the number of cards in hand (such as a {{C|Defend (Ironclad)|basic Defend}}, {{C|Anger}}, {{C|Flechettes}}, {{C|Predator}}, {{C|Compile Driver}} with no {{KW|Orb|Orbs}}, or {{C|Crush Joints}}).\n* When the player's hand is completely {{KW|Exhaust|Exhausted}} (such as {{C|Fiend Fire}}, {{C|Sever Soul}} with only {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}} in hand, or {{C|True Grit}} with only 1 other card in hand).\n* When the player's hand is completely discarded and no new cards are drawn as a result of the same effect (such as by playing {{C|Unload}} with only non-{{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} in hand, or {{C|Survivor}} with only 1 other card in hand).\n** However, this doesn't work if one or more of the cards that were discarded is {{C|Reflex}}.\n* When a card will draw card(s) but then remove the same amount of cards from the hand during its effect.\n** Playing {{C|Warcry}} while it's the only card in hand (it will draw a card, immediately add it to the top of the draw pile, then the player's hand is empty and {{R|Unceasing Top}} will trigger and draw it again).\n** Playing {{C|Dagger Throw}} or {{C|Prepared}} while it's the only card in hand (it will draw a card, immediately discard it, then the player's hand is empty and {{R|Unceasing Top}} will trigger).\n*** Again, this doesn't work if one or more of the cards that were discarded is {{C|Reflex}}.\n\n==== Scenarios That Do Not Trigger {{R|Unceasing Top}} ====\n* When the player's hand is briefly empty in the middle of playing {{C|Calculated Gamble}}, {{C|Storm of Steel}}, or {{C|Reboot}}.\n* Playing a card which draws cards (such as {{C|Quick Slash}} or {{C|Skim}}) when it is the only card in the player's hand.\n* Playing a self-damaging card (like {{C|Bloodletting}}) when it is the only card in hand while also possessing {{R|Runic Cube}} (a card will be drawn by {{R|Runic Cube}} first, so the hand will not be empty).\n* Playing a card that {{KW|Exhaust|Exhausts}} while {{C|Dark Embrace}} is in play.\n* When the last card in hand is also the 10th card play needed to trigger {{R|Ink Bottle}} (a card is drawn from {{R|Ink Bottle}} and {{R|Unceasing Top}} does not trigger).\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Unceasing Top}} can be the source of infinite combos that would otherwise lack the card draw necessary to work (such as reducing the player's deck down to only 0-cost cards like {{C|Anger}}, {{C|Slice}}, or {{C|FTL}}). This involves having a deck with a very low average {{KW|Energy}} cost, and/or [[Exhaust]] synergies to thin the deck during combat.\n* {{R|Unceasing Top}} is best when combined with a lot of {{KW|Energy}} to continue playing cards as they're drawn.\n** Energy relics like {{R|Sundial}} and {{R|Ice Cream}} give the player the ability to play more cards.\n** An {{R|Unceasing Top}} turn can be fuelled by occasionally drawing cards that provide {{KW|Energy}} (such as {{C|Bloodletting}}, {{C|Concentrate}}, {{C|TURBO}}, and {{C|Deus Ex Machina}}).\n* Cards that can help quickly empty the player's hand (such as {{C|Fiend Fire}} and {{C|Unload}}) can be used to start triggering {{R|Unceasing Top}} and get rid of expensive cards without needing to play them.\n* {{R|Medical Kit}} can allow {{R|Unceasing Top}} to continue drawing cards when an {{KW|Unplayable}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Status|Status}} is drawn (such as {{C|Wound}} or {{C|Dazed}}).\n* {{R|Blue Candle}} can allow {{R|Unceasing Top}} to continue drawing cards when an {{KW|Unplayable}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} is drawn (such as {{C|Injury}} or {{C|Regret}}).\n** {{C|Necronomicurse}} will return to the player's hand when {{KW|Exhaust|Exhausted}} however, and will properly stop {{R|Unceasing Top}} from being triggered unless it can be discarded in some other way (such as {{P|Gambler's Brew}}).\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{KW|Unplayable}} cards (such as {{C|Reflex}}, {{C|Tactician}}, {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Status|Statuses}}, and {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}}) can prevent the player from triggering {{R|Unceasing Top}} if they have no way to get rid of them.\n* An {{R|Unceasing Top}} turn may be forced to end if an expensive card is drawn (such as {{C|Bludgeon}}, {{C|Nightmare}}, {{C|Sunder}}, and {{C|Ragnarok}}), or if an X-cost card is drawn (such as {{C|Whirlwind}}, {{C|Skewer}}, {{C|Reinforced Body}}, and {{C|Conjure Blade}}).\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Unceasing Top}} is a reference to the movie [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inception Inception] where the character Dom Cobb uses a spinning top to determine whether he is in a dream or not.\n** {{R|Unceasing Top}} is the focus of the [[Custom Mode]] modifier '''Inception''', which replaces the player's starting relic with {{R|Unceasing Top}}.\n** Using the seed <code>INCEPTI0N</code> or <code>INCEPTION</code> previously replaced the player's starting relic with {{R|Unceasing Top}}. This was removed after the [[Custom Mode]] modifier was introduced.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 52: Release Date Price Increase'''\n** Fixed issue where cards from Unceasing Top were offset on the far left if using a controller.\n* '''Weekly Patch 29: TURBO'''\n** We added special runs that you can play by entering specific seeds:\n*** INCEPTI0N Start with Unceasing Top ''(it's a dream come true!)''\n** Unceasing Top no longer infinitely spins when you are out of cards (hand/discard/draw pile)\n* '''Weekly Patch 23: Testing Continues...'''\n** Fixed instance where Unceasing Top wouldn't draw cards.\n* '''Weekly Patch 20: Immolate'''\n** Unceasing Top relic + Queueing End Turn should now function properly together.\n** Unceasing Top relic no longer triggers after Statuses and Curses autoplay.\n* '''Weekly Patch 19: Seed'''\n** End of turn cards (curses & statuses) are no longer \"played\". Should fix issues with Unceasing Top.\n* '''Weekly Patch 6: We're Back!'''\n** Unceasing Top now clarifies that it's during your turn only.\n** Unceasing Top now triggers 0.2 seconds faster.\n** Cards now teleport instantly (logically, not visually) fixing race conditions with Headbutt and Unceasing Top.\n* '''Weekly Patch 1: The First of Many!'''\n** Unceasing Top is now Rare (was Uncommon)\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2541":{"pageid":2541,"ns":0,"title":"War Paint","revisions":[{"revid":50830,"parentid":37290,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = War Paint\n|Image       = WarPaint.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Upon pick up, {{KW|Upgrade}} 2 random {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}}.\n|Flavor      = In the past, Ironclads would create wards using enchanted war paint before charging into battle.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Upgrade}}{{R|War Paint}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|War Paint}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|War Paint}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|War Paint}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|War Paint}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|War Paint}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|War Paint}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|War Paint}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|War Paint}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|War Paint}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|War Paint}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n* {{R|War Paint}} can be removed by {{E|N'loth}} with no penalty to the player's cards that were upgraded by the relic.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Despite working in a similar way to {{R|Whetstone}}, {{R|War Paint}} does not share its [[Whetstone#Spawn Restrictions|spawning restriction]] of being unable to be acquired from \"Obtain Relic\" events.\n** E.g. {{R|War Paint}} can be acquired from the {{Frame|Search}} option in the {{E|Dead Adventurer}} event and as a reward from {{R|Calling Bell}}, while {{R|Whetstone}} cannot.\n* Many cultures throughout history, and some Indigenous cultures today, use war paint as a pre-battle ritual to apply designs and patterns to their skin.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 14: Bullet Time'''\n** Darkstone Periapt and War Paint now have flavor descriptions.\n* '''Weekly Patch 4: Full speed ahead!'''\n** Adding new relic War Paint.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]}}}}